 mtHsp70-dependent manner to induce apoptosis. Cell Death Differ. 24, 747&#x2013;758 (2017).</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">28338658</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pmc">5384031</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1038/cdd.2017.3</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Kreimendahl, S. &amp; Rassow, J. The mitochondrial outer membrane protein Tom70-mediator in protein traffic, membrane contact sites and innate immunity. Int. J. Mol. Sci. 21, 7262 (2020).</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">33019591</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pmc">7583919</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.3390/ijms21197262</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Lenhard, S. et al. The Orf9b protein of SARS-CoV-2 modulates mitochondrial protein biogenesis. J. Cell Biol. 222, e202303002 (2023).</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">37682539</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="pmc">10491932</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1083/jcb.202303002</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference><Reference><Citation>Liu, X.-Y., Wei, B., Shi, H.-X., Shan, Y.-F. &amp; Wang, C. Tom70 mediates activation of interferon regulatory factor 3 on mitochondria. Cell Res. 20, 994&#x2013;1011 (2010).</Citation><ArticleIdList><ArticleId IdType="pubmed">20628368</ArticleId><ArticleId IdType="doi">10.1038/cr.2010.103</ArticleId></ArticleIdList></Reference></ReferenceList></PubmedData></PubmedArticle><PubmedArticle><MedlineCitation Status="MEDLINE" Owner="NLM" IndexingMethod="Manual"><PMID Version="1">25784053</PMID><DateCompleted><Year>2015</Year><Month>12</Month><Day>17</Day></DateCompleted><DateRevised><Year>2018</Year><Month>11</Month><Day>13</Day></DateRevised><Article PubModel="Print-Electronic"><Journal><ISSN IssnType="Electronic">1545-4509</ISSN><JournalIssue CitedMedium="Internet"><Volume>84</Volume><PubDate><Year>2015</Year></PubDate></JournalIssue><Title>Annual review of biochemistry</Title><ISOAbbreviation>Annu Rev Biochem</ISOAbbreviation></Journal><ArticleTitle>The biology of proteostasis in aging and disease.</ArticleTitle><Pagination><StartPage>435</StartPage><EndPage>464</EndPage><MedlinePgn>435-64</MedlinePgn></Pagination><ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1146/annurev-biochem-060614-033955</ELocationID><Abstract><AbstractText>Loss of protein homeostasis (proteostasis) is a common feature of aging and disease that is characterized by the appearance of nonnative protein aggregates in various tissues. Protein aggregation is routinely suppressed by the proteostasis network (PN), a collection of macromolecular machines that operate in diverse ways to maintain proteome integrity across subcellular compartments and between tissues to ensure a healthy life span. Here, we review the composition, function, and organizational properties of the PN in the context of individual cells and entire organisms and discuss the mechanisms by which disruption of the PN, and related stress response pathways, contributes to the initiation and progression of disease. We explore emerging evidence that disease susceptibility arises from early changes in the composition and activity of the PN and propose that a more complete understanding of the temporal and spatial properties of the PN will enhance our ability to develop effective treatments for protein conformational diseases.</AbstractText></Abstract><AuthorList CompleteYN="Y"><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Labbadia</LastName><ForeName>Johnathan</ForeName><Initials>J</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Molecular Biosciences, Rice Institute for Biomedical Research, Northwestern University, Evanston, Illinois 60208; email: r-morimoto@northwestern.edu.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Morimoto</LastName><ForeName>Richard I</ForeName><Initials>RI</Initials></Author></AuthorList><Language>eng</Language><GrantList CompleteYN="Y"><Grant><GrantID>P50 GM081892</GrantID><Acronym>GM</Acronym><Agency>NIGMS NIH 